      "math_academy_id": "q-342598",
      "sequence_position": 4,
      "problem": "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $5$ | $3$ | $-2$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(4)={g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4)+\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(4)=2,$ $g(4)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{2},$ and ${f}^{′}(2)=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(4) & ={g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4)+\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2+\\frac{(-1)}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{(-1)}{5} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
